-- title:
--   Raynaud quotient has twice the height of A
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $p$ is a prime and $M$ a non-zero natural number with $p \mid M$ (`hpM`), $p^2 \nmid M$ (`hpM2`), $M/p$ non-zero, and $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ a subgroup containing every unit that becomes $1$ in $(\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^\times$ (`hHp`). Further, $Pl$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ a non-unit of $Pl$ (`hPl`, the predicate `LiesOverPrime`), whose residue field has characteristic $p$ and is algebraically closed, and `hj` records that the $q$-expansion `jqModC ℚ` of $j$ lies in the $q$-expansion function field of $\mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbb{Z})$.
--
--   The geometric input consists of: an integral model $\mathfrak{X}$ at $p$ (`XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj`) for the two curves `toBase p (ΓM M H) hj` and `toBase p (ΓN p M H hpM) hj` over $\operatorname{Spec}(R_p)$, where $R_p$ is the localisation of $\mathbb{Q}$'s base ring at $p$ used throughout; level data $\Lambda$ (`JHNeronObjectAtP.LevelData p M H hpM Pl`), consisting of a scheme $\Lambda.X$ over `base p` with a relative group law $\Lambda.L$ and identifications of its generic and special sections with $J_{H'}(M/p)$ and with the degree-zero divisor class group of the special fibre function field; and a Néron object $O$ (`JHNeronObjectAtP p M H hpM Pl hPl Λ`) for $J_H(M) = \mathrm{Pic}^0_{\overline{\mathbb{Q}}}(X_H(M))$, that is, a smooth separated group scheme $O.G \to$ `base p` with relative group law $O.L$, an identification `O.pts` of $J_H(M)$ with its generic sections, Hecke morphisms, and a toric rank `O.toricRank`.
--
--   Two representability hypotheses are imposed. `hrep` asserts that the designation $(O.G, O.g)$ with the unit section of $O.L$ as zero section represents the relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of `toBase p (ΓM M H) hj` rigidified along $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$, for the sub-Picard condition `algEquivZeroCut` (fibrewise algebraic equivalence to zero); `hrepΛ` asserts the same for $(\Lambda.X, \Lambda.f)$ relative to `toBase p (ΓN p M H hpM) hj` rigidified along $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$ followed by $\mathfrak{X}.\pi$.
--
--   The local base is a ring $R_h$: a henselian local domain which is a discrete valuation ring, equipped with a faithful algebra structure on $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ such that every element of $R_h$ maps into $Pl$ (`hRA`) and the maximal ideal of $R_h$ consists exactly of the elements of $Pl$-valuation $<1$ (`hRloc`), together with an algebra map $R_h \to \mathbb{Z}/p$ whose kernel is again the set of elements of $Pl$-valuation $<1$ (`hres`). A set $S \subseteq \mathbb{N}$ and a unit $d \in (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ are given, the latter with the hypothesis `hd` that the image of $d$ in $\mathbb{Z}/(M/p)$ equals $p$.
--
--   The finite-part witness is a $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{G}$ over $R_h$ of height $h$ (a system of finite free $R_h$-Hopf algebras `𝒢.level v` of rank $p^{vh}$ with surjective transition maps whose kernels are the relevant torsion ideals), an additive map $\Delta$ from the group of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $\mathcal{G}$ to $J_H(M)$, and a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-linear map $e$ between the Tate modules (sequences $(x_n)$ with $p^n x_n = 0$ and $p x_{n+1} = x_n$) of these two groups, subject to: `hΔinj`, injectivity of $\Delta$; `hΔlev`, that for each $v$ the image under $\Delta$ of the level-$v$ points is exactly `O.finPts (p^v)`, the subgroup of $J_H(M)$ generated by those $p^v$-torsion classes whose point of $O$ extends over the place in the sense of the predicate `ExtendsToPlace`; `hΔgal`, equivariance of $\Delta$ for $\tau \in \mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ and any $R_h$-algebra automorphism $\tau'$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ agreeing with $\tau$; `hΔhecke`, that for every set $S$ and every generator $g$ of [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13) there are $R_h$-bialgebra endomorphisms $\varphi_v$ of the levels, compatible with the transition maps, inducing `genOpH M H S g` on $J_H(M)$ through $\Delta$; `he`, that $e$ is computed coordinatewise by $\Delta$; `heinj`, injectivity of $e$; `herange`, that a Tate-module element of $J_H(M)$ lies in the range of $e$ precisely when its $n$-th coordinate lies in `O.finPts (p^n)` for all $n$; `hegal`, that $e$ intertwines the Tate-module action of $R_h$-automorphisms of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ on $\mathcal{G}$ with `JH.tateGaloisRep`; `hsat`, saturation of the range of $e$ under division by $p$; `hcoker`, a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-linear isomorphism of the cokernel of $e$ with $\mathbb{Z}_p^{O.toricRank}$; and `htor`, that every element of `O.toricPts (p^v)` (the subgroup generated by the toric points `O.toricPoint`) lies in the image of the level-$v$ points under $\Delta$.
--
--   The Raynaud quotient data is a $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{B}$ over $R_h$ of height $h_B$ together with $R_h$-bialgebra maps $\psi_v : \mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\,v \to \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$, and natural numbers satisfying $h = O.\mathrm{toricRank} + h_B$ (`hhB`) and $h_B = 2h'$ for some $h'$ (`hhB2`). The maps $\psi$ satisfy: `hψt`, compatibility with the transition maps; `hψker`, that the pullback of a level-$v$ point of $\mathcal{G}$ along $\psi_v$ is trivial exactly when its image under $\Delta$ lies in `O.toricPts (p^v)`; `hψsurj`, that every level-$v$ point of $\mathcal{B}$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ arises by pullback along $\psi_v$; `hψred`, that if the $\psi_v$-pullback of a point $x$ reduces to the identity, in the sense that the $Pl$-valuation of $x$ evaluated at each algebra element minus its counit is $<1$, then $x$ itself does; and `hperiod`, that for every $v$, every $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of $Pl$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, every $p^v$-torsion class $z$ of the degree-zero divisor class group of `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` and every level-$v$ point $y$ with $\Delta y = \sigma \cdot z - z$, the $\psi_v$-pullback of $y$ reduces to the identity in that sense.
--
--   The model compatibilities are a ring map $\rho_h : R_p \to R_h$ and morphisms $\iota_v : \operatorname{Spec}(\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v) \to O.G$, subject to: `hρh`, compatibility of $\rho_h$ with the algebra maps into $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; `hιbase`, that $\iota_v$ followed by $O.g$ factors as $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the structure map of $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ followed by $\operatorname{Spec}\rho_h$; `hιcl`, that the resulting map to the fibre product of $O.g$ and $\operatorname{Spec}\rho_h$ is a closed immersion; `hιp`, that $\iota_v$ is annihilated by multiplication by $p^v$ for $O.L$; `hιpts`, that the point of $O$ attached to $\Delta$ of a level-$v$ point $x$ is $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the algebra map of $x$ followed by $\iota_v$; `hιmul`, that on $B$-points (for any $R_h$-algebra $B$) this description is additive for the group law $O.L$; `hιt`, compatibility of $\iota$ with the transition maps; `hιhecke`, that for every $S$ and every generator $g$ there are level endomorphisms $\varphi_v$, compatible with transitions, which induce the Hecke morphism `O.hecke S g` through $\iota$ and induce `genOpH M H S g` through $\Delta$; and `hιfin`, that the comparison morphism $j_v$ from $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v)$ into the fibre product over $\operatorname{Spec} R_h$ of the $p^v$-kernel of $O.L$ is both an open and a closed immersion and its image on points contains every point lying over the closed point of $R_h$.
--
--   The special-fibre data consists of a morphism $\sigma_p : \operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{Z}/p) \to$ `base p` with `hσp` identifying it with the reduction of $\rho_h$; morphisms $\iota p_v : \operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{Z}/p \otimes_{R_h} \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v) \to O.G \times_{\mathrm{base}\,p} \operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{Z}/p)$ whose two projections are prescribed by `hιp₁` (inclusion of the right factor followed by $\iota_v$) and `hιp₂` (the structure map); a pair $q : \mathrm{Fin}\,2 \to$ morphisms over $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{Z}/p)$ from the base change of $O.g$ along $\sigma_p$ to that of $\Lambda.f$; an algebra structure on the residue field of $Pl$ over $\mathbb{Z}/p$ with `hfac` identifying $\operatorname{Spec}$ of that algebra map followed by $\sigma_p$ with `resPt Pl` followed by $\Lambda.\sigma_A$; `hqmul`, that each $q_i$ is a homomorphism for the base-changed group laws on $T$-points; `hqbc`, that the two components `O.abqFibre i` agree with $q_i$ after the canonical base-change maps along the residue field; and `hjB`, injectivity for each $v$ of the base change $\mathbb{Z}/p \otimes \psi_v$ (namely of `Bialgebra.TensorProduct.map (BialgHom.id (ZMod p) (ZMod p)) (ψ v)`).
--
--   Finally, a $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{A}$ over the base ring of `base p` of height $h_\Lambda$ is given, with an algebra structure of $\mathbb{Z}/p$ over that base ring such that $\sigma_p$ is $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the corresponding algebra map (`hσp'`), and with morphisms $\iota'_v : \operatorname{Spec}((\mathcal{A} \otimes \mathbb{Z}/p).\mathrm{level}\,v) \to \Lambda.X \times_{\mathrm{base}\,p} \operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{Z}/p)$ satisfying: `hι'base`, that $\iota'_v$ lies over $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{Z}/p)$ via the structure map; `hι'cl`, that each $\iota'_v$ is a closed immersion; `hι'p`, that $\iota'_v$ is annihilated by $p^v$ for the base-changed group law $\Lambda.L$ and that the induced morphism into the $p^v$-kernel is an isomorphism; `hι'mul`, additivity of $\iota'_v$ on $B$-points for any $\mathbb{Z}/p$-algebra $B$; and `hι't`, compatibility of $\iota'$ with the transition maps of $\mathcal{A} \otimes \mathbb{Z}/p$.
--
--   Under these hypotheses the conclusion is the single numerical identity
--   $$h_B = 2\,h_\Lambda,$$
--   that is, the height of the $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{B}$ equals twice the height of $\mathcal{A}$.
--
--   This is the height bookkeeping of the Deligne–Rapoport analysis of $X_H(M)$ at a prime $p$ exactly dividing $M$: after the toric part of the $p$-divisible group attached to the finite part of $J_H(M)$ has been divided out, the resulting group $\mathcal{B}$ has height twice that of the $p$-divisible group of the level-$(M/p)$ object on the special fibre. It is used by the two statements producing idempotents and bialgebra maps for the base-changed Raynaud quotient, where the numerical relation between the two heights is needed but cannot be obtained inside their own frames.
